            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

During the blower door test, we identified that your house has some air leakage associated with the following locations:



** The outside doors - these doors are not sealing properly (can seal with weatherstripping).

** Some of the electrical outlets in exterior walls of the house - e.g. the one near the entrance door. Foam wall plate gaskets can be installed behind the plates.

** 5" flue of the oil furnace



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $ 190 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 5.7 GJ. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.



</airSeal>
                <foundation>

The basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 1 inch (R4) rigid foam, extended polystrene insulation. I have modeled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 3.5 inches (R14) of batt insulation. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 1 inch (R4) rigid foam, extended polystrene insulation from inside and 1.5 inches (R7.5) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation on the outside. I have modelled adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam, extruded polystyrene insulation, as well as 2x4 framing with 3.5 inches (R14) of batt insulation to the pony walls. 



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation and 2x4 framing with 3.5 inches (R14) of fiberglass batt insulation to the basement and pony walls, you could potentially save $ 830 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 25 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, could potentially save $ 1,100 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with a 83% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current propane furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 1,570 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.6 GJ, 



By installing 97% AFUE condensing furnace with an ECM fan blower, you could qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with an ENERGY STAR certified propane-fired furnace or boiler. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant.



Note: You can still get GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate if you were to install 96% AFUE condensing furnace but you will not be eligible for Canada Greener Homes Grant.</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

- In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

- If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

- Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

- All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

- It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

--- installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

--- an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

--- the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

- All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.2 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace vent up a chimney and is susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house: 

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2021 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $1.25 per liter 

Propane - $0.73 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $6.00 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Abdul Mohammed, Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
